agent agent   5124 Nov 11 14:27 reference_query_plan.md\n-rw------- 1 agent agent  82498 Nov 11 14:27 reference_query_plan_agent_claude_20261111_142659.log\n-rw------- 1 agent agent  23626 Nov 11 14:27 reference_query_plan_prompt.md\n-rw------- 1 agent agent    266 Nov 11 14:27 reference_query_plan_validation_feedback.json\n-rw------- 1 agent agent    280 Nov 11 14:27 reference_query_plan_validation_feedback.md\n-rw------- 1 agent agent   3335 Nov 11 14:29 reference_retrieval.log\n-rw------- 1 agent agent   7440 Nov 11 14:29 references.bib\n-rw------- 1 agent agent  24941 Nov 11 14:29 references_provenance.json\nlrwxrwxrwx 1 agent agent     16 Nov 11 14:26 results -> ../investigation\n-rw------- 1 agent agent    266 Nov 11 14:31 strategy_validation_feedback.json\n-rw------- 1 agent agent    272 Nov 11 14:31 strategy_validation_feedback.md\nlrwxrwxrwx 1 agent agent      7 Nov 11 14:26 task -> ../task\n# ICLR 2026 Venue Brief\n\nUse this brief to tailor the manuscript for the International Conference on Learning\nRepresentations 2026 submission.\n\n## Hard Constraints\n- Target venue: ICLR 2026.\n- Submission type: conference paper.\n- Body-page budget: 8 to 9 pages, excluding references. A body of 8 pages counts only when its final page is at least 75% full; a shorter or sparser body fails validation.\n- Review model: double-blind. The submission must be fully anonymized.\n- Omit author names, affiliations, acknowledgements, and funding statements from the manuscript.\n- Do not include self-identifying links such as personal repositories, lab pages, or internal URLs.\n- Refer to your own prior work in the third person; never write phrasing that reveals authorship.\n- Include a separate `LLM Usage` section, which may appear in the appendix, describing the precise role\n  of LLMs in research ideation and writing. Significant LLM use must be disclosed; nondisclosure can\n  cause desk rejection.\n- The paper must satisfy the ICLR template and formatting rules selected by `--paper-format iclr`.\n\n## Audience\n- Machine learning researchers working on representation learning, optimization, architectures, and\n  empirical methodology.\n- Readers expect a clearly stated contribution, honest baselines, controlled comparisons, ablations\n  that isolate the mechanism, and explicit statements of scope and limitations.\n- Reviewers reward reproducibility and calibrated claims far more than headline numbers.\n\n## Framing Priorities\n- Lead with the research question and the specific claim the evidence supports.\n- State the contribution precisely in the abstract and introduction: what is new, what it improves,\n  and under which conditions.\n- Motivate the method mechanistically. Explain why it should work, not only that it does.\n- Support the central claim with controlled comparisons against meaningful baselines.\n- Use ablations to attribute the gain to the proposed component rather than to incidental changes.\n- Report variance across seeds or runs whenever the artifacts provide it.\n- Be explicit about the regime studied and where the result is not expected to hold.\n\n## Manuscript Guidance\n- Prefer a title and abstract that name the mechanism and the setting, not a generic improvement claim.\n- In the introduction, make the contribution list concrete and verifiable against the artifacts.\n- In the method section, give enough detail to reimplement: objective, architecture, and the\n  hyperparameters that matter.\n- In the results section, foreground controlled comparisons, ablations, sensitivity, and failure modes.\n- Include a limitations discussion that a reviewer would otherwise raise.\n- Figures must meet top-tier conference standards in both design (clean typography, consistent colors,\n  proper labels and legends, high resolution) and scientific content (clear message, no filler).\n- The template is single-column: size figures for the full text width and keep axis labels legible.\n\n## Anti-Goals\n- Do not deanonymize the submission in any way.\n- Do not overclaim generality beyond the settings actually evaluated.\n- Do not present a benchmark delta as a mechanistic insight without evidence connecting the two.\n- Do not omit baselines or ablations that the grounded artifacts already support.\n- Do not describe results as state of the art unless the comparison in the artifacts establishes it.\n- Do not pad the paper to fill the page budget.\n\n## Decision Rule\n- When venue framing and grounded evidence conflict, follow the grounded evidence.\n- Use this brief to choose emphasis, terminology, and narrative framing, not to invent stronger claims\n  than the run supports.\n# Paper strategy (standalone prompt family, ICLR 2026 single-column, 8–9 body pages)\n\n**Status:** ready. **Edit mode:** off (no prior manuscript) → `prior_paper_story_status: rejected` is a formal \"fresh framing\" value only.\n\n## Provisional title\nReporting Scores After Prompt Search: A Selection-Aware Posterior Interval from a Hierarchical Latent-Difficulty Model\n\n## Central question\nWhen a configuration is chosen on a finite benchmark sample and its search-time score is reported, how large is the optimism, what controls it, and can a selection-aware interval built only from the search-time evaluation log report fresh-question accuracy more reliably?\n\n## Central thesis\nThe naive best-of-search score with a Wilson interval is biased upward and too narrow when many configurations are compared on few questions. An empirical-Bayes hierarchical logistic latent-difficulty posterior interval, fitted to all logged (question, config, outcome) triples and agnostic to the searcher, lowers the mean 90%-level interval score relative to naive, Bonferroni-Wilson and question-resampling reporters in the sealed final evaluation (outcome **qualified**, status completed). It retains residual bias and sub-nominal coverage in specific real-data regimes, so the claim is conditional.\n\n## Why this framing\n- Final evaluation: mean interval score 0.1449 (proposed) vs 0.2202 naive, 0.1974 Bonferroni, 0.2461 resample; coverage 0.929; null-control coverage 0.929; constant-world error 0.0 (`final.metric.*`, `final.qualification.*`).\n- No `downstream_study` block → no downstream claims. Search: 3 of 3 attempts scored, 0 unscored; the frozen selection is the EB-IRT variant (-0.1480 search score; alternatives -0.1519 and -0.1562 from one evaluation each, not a significance ranking). Only the frozen winner has a final evaluation.\n- The interesting content is heterogeneity across cases: large gains on synthetic and small-N real worlds; parity with naive on real halving (P4) and large N (P6); sub-nominal coverage there; the resample reporter is slightly better on several real exhaustive cases but very poor under halving. Honest reporting of this beats a \"wins everywhere\" story and beats a chronological campaign story.\n- Rejected framings: (a) search-campaign chronology of three variants (variants are close in score; no final evidence for the two losers); (b) a broad \"gap in LLM prompt tuning\" claim (only one 0.5B model, two searchers, evaluator-owned worlds were tested); (c) a pure-theory framing (no theory results in artifacts).\n\n## Method groups\n1. Baselines: naive Wilson; Bonferroni-widened Wilson; question-resampling optimism reporter.\n2. Proposed (frozen selection): EB hierarchical logistic latent-difficulty posterior interval (logit P = a_config − b_question, EB variances, Laplace draws, Gauss-Hermite over fresh difficulties, 5/95% quantiles, kappa calibration, degenerate-world and time guards).\n3. Variants (search-plane only): parametric-bootstrap pivot calibration (narrow, coverage 0.77); config-by-subject interaction (coverage 0.8925, no gain).\n4. Controls: synthetic null N1, real permuted-config null N2, constant-zero Z1.\n\n## Results views\n1. Aggregate final table with spread over 8 final seeds.\n2. Optimism gap (naive signed bias, 0.0366 aggregate) vs N, K, searcher, world; proposed reporter's residual bias.\n3. Per-case interval score and coverage vs the 0.90 nominal level.\n4. Control behaviour.\n5. Search-stage width-vs-coverage comparison (kept separate).\n6. Open items: whether the gap changes comparative conclusions is not directly tested; say so.\n\n## Figures (all full text width; template textwidth 5.5 in; PDF + 300-DPI PNG; PDF referenced in LaTeX)\n- `overview_schematic` (required, `generate-concept-figure`, page 1–2): problem and reporter pipeline.\n- `per_case_interval_score` (required, matplotlib, 2 panels ≤5 cases, 4 series; handle resample P2 outlier by annotation/broken axis).\n- `bias_and_coverage` (required, matplotlib, 2 panels, 2 series).\n- `width_vs_n` (optional, ~0.7 width; move to supplement if unneeded).\n- Tables: final aggregate; search-stage variants.\n\n## Body-page use\nProblem formalization and interval score; estimator with reimplementable detail and ignorability motivation; controlled comparison with baselines; bias/coverage diagnostics; controls; sizeable limitations section. No padding.\n\n## Supplement\nEstimator details/hyperparameters; per-case × per-seed tables; search-stage variants; evaluation protocol and provenance (pinned MMLU-Pro and Qwen2.5-0.5B-Instruct revisions, seeds 9101–9108, measured compute); LLM-usage disclosure.\n\n## Limits to preserve\nOne 0.5B model, MMLU-Pro, 24 prompt configs, exhaustive and successive-halving searchers, evaluator-owned synthetic worlds; no DSPy/MIPRO, no adaptive Ladder-style reporting; outcome tensor shipped with the task (not regenerated here); kappa calibrated on a local development harness; per-case diagnostics are `unverified` in trust; qualification gate diagnostics beyond outcome are unknown. Reference list: most bib entries are irrelevant (medical etc.) — cite only the adaptive-data-analysis entries; starter-literature works need verified addition before citing.\n\n## Avoid\nChronology headings (campaign, milestones, iterations), pipeline-native terms (`combined_score`, \"eligible\", \"correctness rate\", \"rubric\"), SOTA / \"guarantees coverage\" / generality claims.\nfigures:","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"50ad42a8-2b3f-48e6-bd4a-8d4c069e45a9","uuid":"97897fbe-8eab-4e2c-9706-5e1d0804af02","timestamp":"2026-11-11T14:31:56.603Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"total 812\ndrwx------ 5 agent agent   4096 Nov 11 14:31 .\ndrwx------ 8 agent agent   4096 Nov 11 14:26 ..\n-rw------- 1 agent agent    751 Nov 11 14:26 .agentic_paper_workspace.json\ndrwx------ 3 agent agent   4096 Nov 11 14:27 .claude\nprw------- 1 agent agent      0 Nov 11 14:31 .paper_stream_20261111_142659.fifo\n-rw------- 1 agent agent      0 Nov 11 14:26 .paper_two_stage_enabled\n-rw------- 1 agent agent   3685 Nov 11 14:31 VENUE.md\ndrwx------ 2 agent agent   4096 Nov 11 14:26 figures\nlrwxrwxrwx 1 agent agent     16 Nov 11 14:27 global_memory -> ../investigation\n-rw------- 1 agent agent   8023 Nov 11 14:27 global_memory_recent.json\n-rw------- 1 agent agent   3914 Nov 11 14:27 global_memory_summary.md\n-rw-r--r-- 1 agent agent    662 Nov 11 14:31 memory.md\n-rw-r--r-- 1 agent agent  26973 Nov 10 13:47 paper.bst\n-rw-r--r-- 1 agent agent   8934 Nov 10 13:47 paper.sty\n-rw------- 1 agent agent      0 Nov 11 14:31 paper_agent_claude_20261111_142659.log\n-rw------- 1 agent agent 158232 Nov 11 14:27 paper_data.json\ndrwx------ 3 agent agent   4096 Nov 11 14:27 paper_diagnostics\n-rw------- 1 agent agent      0 Nov 11 14:26 paper_diagnostics_20261111_142659_47812.log\n-rw------- 1 agent agent   2526 Nov 11 14:31 paper_events_20261111_142659.jsonl\n-rw------- 1 agent agent    795 Nov 11 14:26 paper_prep_20261111_142659.json\n-rw------- 1 agent agent  58211 Nov 11 14:31 paper_prompt.md\n-rw-r--r-- 1 agent agent  19463 Nov 11 14:31 paper_strategy.json\n-rw-r--r-- 1 agent agent   5418 Nov 11 14:31 paper_strategy.md\n-rw------- 1 agent agent 259851 Nov 11 14:31 paper_strategy_agent_claude_20261111_142659.log\n-rw------- 1 agent 