3-4b30-9330-eb61fca26ca7","timestamp":"2026-11-11T14:31:58.486Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"{\n  \"status\": \"ready\",\n  \"selected_prompt_family\": \"paper_standalone\",\n  \"recommended_title\": \"Reporting Scores After Prompt Search: A Selection-Aware Posterior Interval from a Hierarchical Latent-Difficulty Model\",\n  \"central_question\": \"When a configuration is chosen on a finite benchmark sample and its search-time score is then reported, how large is the optimism of that score, which factors control it, and can a selection-aware interval built only from the search-time evaluation log give a more reliable report of fresh-question accuracy?\",\n  \"central_thesis\": \"The naive best-of-search score with a Wilson interval is biased upward and too narrow when many configurations are compared on few questions; an empirical-Bayes hierarchical logistic latent-difficulty posterior interval, fitted to the full logged (question, config, outcome) triples and agnostic to the searcher, lowers the mean 90%-level interval score relative to naive, Bonferroni-Wilson, and question-resampling reporters in the sealed final evaluation (qualified outcome), but it keeps residual positive bias and sub-nominal coverage in specific real-data regimes (successive halving, large N), so the claim is conditional on the studied worlds.\",\n  \"narrative_rationale\": \"The final evaluation completed with outcome 'qualified' (final.qualification.outcome), mean interval score 0.1449 vs 0.2202 (naive), 0.1974 (Bonferroni), 0.2461 (resample) (final.metric.*), coverage 0.929 and null-control coverage 0.929. This gives a positive but narrow, conditional story. The task asks about the gap between reported and fresh-question accuracy and a more reliable reporting method; the run answers both through evaluator-owned synthetic worlds with exact truth and cached real Qwen2.5-0.5B-Instruct outcomes on MMLU-Pro under 24 prompt configurations, with exhaustive and successive-halving searchers and null/constant controls. A thesis-first estimator-and-evidence paper beats a campaign-chronology paper: the three model variants evaluated in search are close in score and only the frozen winner has a final evaluation, so they are best presented as a width/coverage trade-off comparison, not as a progression. Per-case results show where the method wins big (synthetic and real exhaustive, small N), where it is merely on par with naive (real halving, large N), and where a simple question-resampling reporter is slightly better (real exhaustive cases) or catastrophically bad (halving) - that heterogeneity is the scientifically interesting content and must be foregrounded for honesty. No downstream_study block exists in paper_data.json, so no downstream-study claims.\",\n  \"prior_paper_story_status\": \"rejected\",\n  \"edit_framing_decision\": \"Edit mode is not enabled (no previous_paper or edit context staged); there is no prior manuscript to preserve, broaden or recenter, so the story is chosen fresh from grounded results.\",\n  \"staged_input_impact\": [\n    \"No staged prior paper or new-program inputs exist; the framing rests only on paper_data.json, task contract, and the winning program source in the investigation directory.\"\n  ],\n  \"method_groups\": [\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Naive best-of-search score with Wilson 90% interval\",\n      \"role\": \"baseline\",\n      \"include_reason\": \"The status-quo reporting practice the task questions; its signed bias (final.metric.naive_signed_bias = 0.0366) quantifies the optimism gap and the incumbent for scoring.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Bonferroni-widened Wilson interval and question-resampling (bootstrap-optimism) reporter\",\n      \"role\": \"baseline\",\n      \"include_reason\": \"Two evaluator-supplied selection-aware alternatives (final.metric.bonferroni_baseline_score, final.metric.resample_baseline_score); needed so the proposed gain is not against a strawman. Resample is better on some real exhaustive cases and fails under halving.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Empirical-Bayes hierarchical logistic latent-difficulty posterior interval (frozen selection)\",\n      \"role\": \"proposed\",\n      \"include_reason\": \"Winner of search (result.best_correct_score = -0.1480) and the evaluated method in the sealed final run. Logit P = a_config - b_question with EB variance estimates, ignorable-selection argument, Laplace draws plus Gauss-Hermite over fresh difficulties, 5/95% quantiles, calibration factor kappa, degenerate-world guards.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Search-stage variants: parametric-bootstrap pivot calibration; config-by-subject interaction random effects\",\n      \"role\": \"ablation\",\n      \"include_reason\": \"Two scored alternatives to the winner (result.top_program_02, result.top_program_03; 3 of 3 attempts scored, 0 unscored). Shows narrower intervals (c02 width 0.085, coverage 0.77) lose on interval score and extra interaction structure (c03) did not beat the plain model. Search-plane only; report as variant comparison, not as final evidence, and not as a significance ranking (single evaluation each).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Negative controls (synthetic null N1, real permuted-config null N2, constant-zero world Z1)\",\n      \"role\": \"supporting\",\n      \"include_reason\": \"Check the reporter does not manufacture signal and handles degenerate worlds (final.metric.null_control_abs_bias, null_control_coverage, constant_control_error = 0.0).\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"results_focus\": [\n    \"Sealed-final aggregate: mean interval score of the proposed reporter vs naive, Bonferroni and resample reporters (final.metric.*), with spread across the 8 final seeds (final.evidence.episodes). State outcome 'qualified' (final.qualification.outcome) with the completed status and 100% replication completion; do not extend into generality.\",\n    \"Optimism gap and its drivers: naive signed bias per case (large at small N=60 ~0.051, smaller at N=200 ~0.022-0.025, ~0.009 at N=600; synthetic ~0.05-0.07; derived from per-case final episodes) versus the proposed reporter's residual bias; discuss N, K, searcher. Compute exact values from final.evidence.episodes; flag that per-case diagnostics have candidate_diagnostics_trust 'unverified'.\",\n    \"Per-case interval score and coverage (synthetic P1,P2; real exhaustive P3,P5,P6; real halving P4) vs 0.90 nominal: large gains over naive on synthetic and small-N real cases, parity with naive on P4/P6, and sub-nominal coverage on P4 and P6; resample baseline slightly better on some real exhaustive cases and far worse under halving (P2, P4).\",\n    \"Negative controls: null-control coverage 0.929 and absolute bias 0.017 vs naive; constant-zero error 0.0.\",\n    \"Search-stage width-versus-coverage trade-off among three scored model variants (result.top_program_01..03; coverage 0.915 / 0.8925 / 0.77; widths 0.124 / 0.120 / 0.085), kept clearly separate from the final plane.\",\n    \"Does the optimism gap change conclusions? Only supported to the extent that a miscalibrated interval's miss penalty and coverage show reported numbers are unreliable; do not claim ranking-reversal results unless computed from artifacts (none are in paper_data.json).\"\n  ],\n  \"figure_plan\": [\n    \"Fig 1 (overview, page 1-2): conceptual schematic of the selection-aware reporting problem: finite benchmark sample -> search over K configs -> selected config + evaluation log -> reporters (naive Wilson / Bonferroni / resampling / EB-IRT posterior) -> interval for fresh-question accuracy; shows the winner's-curse bias.\",\n    \"Fig 2: per-case interval score (lower better) for four reporters, two panels (synthetic worlds incl. N1; real-LLM worlds incl. N2), grouped bars with seed-to-seed error bars.\",\n    \"Fig 3: two-panel diagnostic: (a) signed bias of naive vs proposed reporter per case; (b) empirical 90% interval coverage per case with nominal line.\",\n    \"Ta