{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01YaTbrqE75k2fkUaVvTBC1x","type":"tool_result","content":"# Memory Log\n## Idea Discoveries\n### 2026-11-11 | step=idea\n- idea_id: c01_eb_irt_posterior_interval\n- summary: Interface is a post-selection interval reporter scored by interval score at 0.90 (optimum = 5/95% predictive quantiles of y). Alternatives considered: (1) question-bootstrap optimism/.632 correction (exhaustive only), (2) Efron-style simple shrinkage + widened Wilson, (3) hierarchical IRT posterior (selected; searcher-agnostic since selection is ignorable for the likelihood).\n- evidence: baseline docstring in task/program_0/src/program.py; incumbent combined_score=-0.232 (naive Wilson).\n- rubrics: beats_incumbent_combined_score (metric); hierarchical_irt_fit_on_sparse_log, posterior_quantile_interval_with_calibration, degenerate_world_and_time_guards (implementation)\n- next_action: implement program_1 with local dev harness on resources/search; calibrate kappa; fallback bootstrap optimism for exhaustive searcher.\n- gate=kept; trigger=none; reason=first cycle, no evidence yet to distill.\n### 2026-11-11 | step=implement | attempt_id=c01_eb_irt_posterior_interval_attempt_01 | idea_id=c01_eb_irt_posterior_interval | program_id=program_1\n- summary: Cloned program_0 -> program_1; rewrote program_1/src/program.py (only file changed; program_spec.toml untouched). Implements _fit_irt (bincount-based damped Newton sweeps, EB tau2/s2 updates with Schur-complement Laplace correction, per-subject difficulty means), posterior draws of (a_k, level shift, log s) with hermegauss integration over fresh difficulties, np.quantile 5%/95% interval widened by kappa(N,K,real), degenerate guards (all-zero rule-of-three capped at 0.02, unobserved config, tau2 floor), time.perf_counter fit guard (1.5s), try/except fallback to widened Wilson.\n- limitations: nothing was executed (no tests/dev harness run in this stage); kappa and clamps are untuned priors, not calibrated on the search split. 1-D latent difficulty may be miscalibrated for real MMLU-Pro subject x config interactions. The question-bootstrap secondary option and held-out calibration check were not implemented. A stray empty python heredoc was run once by mistake (no code executed).\n- next_action: harness runs the official evaluation.\n{\n \"hypothesis\": \"The interval score at level 0.90 is minimised by the 5% and 95% quantiles of the predictive distribution of the selected config's fresh-question accuracy y. The naive reporter (best search score + Wilson) is biased upward (winner's curse) and too narrow in the 'many configs, few questions' regime. Searcher-agnostic debiasing should lower the mean interval score: fit a hierarchical logistic latent-difficulty model P(correct)=sigmoid(a_k - b_q), with a_k ~ N(0,tau^2) and b_q ~ N(mu_subject, s^2), to ALL logged (question, config, outcome) triples with tau and s set by empirical Bayes. Because both exhaustive search and successive halving choose configs using only already-observed outcomes, selection is ignorable for the likelihood. The posterior of theta_k = E_b[sigmoid(a_k - b)] for the selected config is therefore valid under either searcher without a searcher-specific correction. Shrinkage toward the pooled mean removes the winner's-curse bias; with tau estimated near 0 it handles the all-configs-equal worlds. Posterior quantiles (Laplace draws of a_k, mu, s, then Gauss-Hermite integration over fresh difficulties) give the interval. Expected result: lower mean interval score than the naive Wilson baseline (combined_score -0.232), with the biggest gain at large K, small N and the halving searcher. Failure mode: real MMLU-Pro outcomes are multi-dimensional (subject x config interactions), so the 1-D model can be miscalibrated there. A held-out-question calibration widening factor and a bootstrap-style fallback are the safeguards.\",\n \"idea_id\": \"c01_eb_irt_posterior_interval\",\n \"implementation_plan\": [\n  \"In program_1/src/program.py, keep the handle(request) python_rpc_v1 interface. reset stores the seed and returns exactly {'ok': True}. report returns exactly estimate, lower and upper, clipped so that 0<=lower<=estimate<=upper<=1 and all values are finite floats.\",\n  \"Decode log.question_idx, log.config_idx, log.outcome and subject_code from base64 little-endian arrays with np.frombuffer (dtypes uint16/uint8/uint8/uint8). Work only with arrays from the request, using no files or network.\",\n  \"Implement _fit_irt(q, c, y, subj, N, K): MAP fit of logit P = a[c] - b[q] by alternating damped Newton steps. Ridge priors a ~ N(0,tau^2), b ~ N(mu_subject, s^2), with a per-subject mean shrunk to the global mean. Update tau^2 and s^2 each outer iteration from the posterior second moments. Include the Laplace/trace correction so tau^2 does not collapse or inflate. Clamp tau^2 to [1e-4, 4].\",\n  \"Work only with sparse triples so the same code handles the exhaustive (N*K entries) and halving (sparse log) searchers. Use vectorised numpy with np.bincount for the per-config and per-question gradient and Hessian diagonals. Cap total outer iterations (about 30) and add a wall-clock guard (about 1.5 s) that returns the best current fit.\",\n  \"Posterior for the selected config: approximate Var(a_k) by the inverse of the diagonal Hessian plus the uncertainty in mu. Draw S (about 400) samples of (a_k, mu_global, log s) with the reset-seeded np.random.default_rng. For each draw compute theta = sum_j w_j * sigmoid(a_k - mu_sub_mix - s*x_j) using np.polynomial.hermite_e.hermegauss nodes, mixing over the empirical subject distribution when subject_code is not all zero.\",\n  \"Report estimate = posterior median or mean of theta, lower = np.quantile(theta, 0.05) and upper = np.quantile(theta, 0.95). Then apply a width-calibration factor kappa >= 1, applied around the estimate, that depends on min(N, evidence), K and whether subject_code is all zero (real vs synthetic).\",\n  \"Degenerate guards. If all outcomes are 0, return estimate 0, lower 0 and an upper bound no greater than a rule-of-three bound (about 3/n_selected_obs, capped, e.g. <=0.02). If the selected config has no observations, fall back to the pooled mean with a wide interval. If tau is estimated at its lower clamp (all configs equal), report a pooled-mean interval.\",\n  \"Build a local dev harness OUTSIDE program_1/src (for example dev/sim.py). It should generate synthetic latent-difficulty worlds across N in {50..2000} and K in {2..64}, plus equal-config worlds, and run both exhaustive and successive-halving searchers. It should also replay ../../task/resources/search/outcomes.npz (24 configs) with both searchers on subsampled question sets, compute the true y from held-out questions, and compute the mean interval score IS=(u-l)+20*max(l-y,0)+20*max(y-u,0). Compare the naive Wilson baseline, the posterior interval without calibration, and the calibrated version.\",\n  \"Use the dev harness to choose kappa and the clamps on the search split only. Check coverage near 0.90 in each (searcher, K, N) stratum. Check that the average per-call time is <=1 s and the worst-case is <=5 s on 4 threads. Set OMP/MKL threads to <=4. Optionally add a held-out-question calibration check inside report (a fit on 80% of questions, scoring the selected config on the other 20%) only if it \nmetric_values.csv\nrun_summary.csv\ntimeseries.csv\nevaluation_id,variant_id,variant_kind,base_program,agent_name,correct,combined_score,overall_pass,primary_metric_name,primary_metric_value,program_dir,results_dir\r\nunknown,unknown,unknown,unknown,unknown,True,-0.1479678140153992,,combined_score,-0.1479678140153992,program_1,analysis\nShell cwd was reset to /workspace/paper","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"50ad42a8-2b3f-48e6-bd4a-8d4c069e45a9","uuid":"7611840d-633a-47a1-a49c-359d4bac61ad","timestamp":"2026-11-11T14:32:52.539Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"# Memo