{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgwKPZhX5mUhUUQghb2ZR","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01335Adc3juqgr5vRe4FT3QN","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /workspace/paper; python3 - <<'E'\ns=open('analysis/make_figures.py').read()\ns=s.replace('ax.set_xscale(\"log\"); ax.set_xticks([60, 200, 600]); ax.set_xticklabels([\"60\", \"200\", \"600\"])','ax.set_xscale(\"log\"); ax.set_xticks([60, 200, 600]); ax.set_xticklabels([\"60\", \"200\", \"600\"]); ax.minorticks_off()')\nopen('analysis/make_figures.py','w').write(s)\np=open('paper.tex').read()\na=\"\\\\emph{Residual bias and miscalibrated width.}\"\nfig=\"\"\"\\\\begin{figure}[t]\n\\\\centering\n\\\\includegraphics[width=0.7\\\\textwidth]{figures/width_vs_n.pdf}\n\\\\caption{Mean interval width on the real exhaustive cases ($K=24$) as the number of benchmark questions grows (mean over eight seeds). EB-IRT is wider than the naive interval by a factor of 1.1--1.3 at each $N$ and much narrower than Bonferroni-Wilson. Naive and Bonferroni widths are replayed from the evaluator's code.}\n\\\\label{fig:width}\n\\\\end{figure}\n\n\"\"\"\nassert a in p\np=p.replace(a,fig+a,1)\np=p.replace(\"The aggregate coverage of $0.929$ is thus\",\"Interval width shrinks with $N$ for all reporters (Figure~\\\\ref{fig:width}): EB-IRT is $0.187$, $0.101$ and $0.065$ wide at $N=60$, $200$, $600$, against $0.167$, $0.088$ and $0.050$ for the naive interval and $0.284$, $0.153$ and $0.088$ for Bonferroni-Wilson. The aggregate coverage of $0.929$ is thus\")\nb=\"\\\\textbf{What it does not support.}\"\nadd=\"\"\"\\\\textbf{How to test a reporter (Q2).} Our results suggest what a reliability test should contain. (a) Worlds with known truth, so that coverage and bias are measured rather than inferred. (b) Interval score together with coverage, since either alone is gamed by extreme widths: Bonferroni-Wilson reaches coverage $0.96$ only by being $48\\\\%$ wider than EB-IRT. (c) Results by regime (search procedure, question budget, world), because the aggregate coverage of $0.929$ conceals cases from $0.83$ to $0.98$. (d) Negative controls: a null world to catch invented signal, a constant world to catch degenerate behaviour. (e) Seed-level uncertainty and paired differences between reporters, since baseline scores vary across seeds by up to $0.025$ in standard deviation while the EB-IRT score varies by $0.004$. (f) Baselines matched to the searcher: against resampling alone, the exhaustive-search verdict (a tie) differs from the halving verdict (a large gain).\n\n\"\"\"\nassert b in p\np=p.replace(b,add+b,1)\nopen('paper.tex','w').write(p)\nE\n/agent-tmp/pyenv/bin/python analysis/make_figures.py; /agent-tmp/pyenv/bin/python -c \"\nw=0.1901/0.1291; print(w, 0.0234,0.0251)\""},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2518,"cache_read_input_tokens":202707,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2518},"output_tokens":36,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"50ad42a8-2b3f-48e6-bd4a-8d4c069e45a9","uuid":"b91e6f33-59a2-42ef-96f4-8e7dfa33412a","timestamp":"2026-11-11T14:43:15.150Z","request_id":"req_011CgwKPY4npZxQApTXxDvjh"}
