{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Dss5ZFvQDey4EE5nBDi3R1","type":"tool_result","content":"Supplementary Information validation failed: Supplementary Information must not import unchecked TeX files\n46:% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.combined_score]]\n98:% @evidence[[source_evidence/reporter_eb_irt/program.py]]\n147:% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.combined_score]]\n157:% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.combined_score]]\n244:% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.combined_score]]\n260:% @evidence[[source_evidence/reporter_eb_irt/program.py]]\n59:Prompt and pipeline optimizers such as DSPy and MIPRO search over instructions, demonstrations and multi-call structures on a benchmark sample \\citep{Khattab2023DSPy,OpsahlOng2024MIPRO}, and in practice the best configuration is often reported with the score it attained on the very sample used to select it. Configuration search is also cast as best-arm identification under a fixed evaluation budget \\citep{Shi2024PromptBAI}. The reported number is the maximum, over many candidates, of noisy estimates, and the candidate that attains it was chosen \\emph{because} its estimate was high. It is therefore a biased estimate of that candidate's accuracy on new questions, and the usual interval around it ignores the selection. Whether comparisons and decisions built on such numbers are trustworthy depends on how large the gap is and on what controls it.\n113:with a width factor $\\kappa(N,K)=\\kappa_0\\,\\big(1+0.25\\min(1,\\sqrt{50/N})\\big)\\big(1+0.03\\log_2(K/2)\\big)$, where $\\kappa_0=1.20$ when the log carries subject codes (real outcomes) and $1.12$ otherwise. The constants are fixed in the source; they are design priors that widen the interval to compensate for ignored hyperparameter uncertainty and misspecification, not values fitted to the final worlds. Over the cases studied $\\kappa$ ranges from 1.43 to 1.63 (Table~\\ref{tab:kappa} in the Supplement), so intervals are roughly 40--60\\% wider than raw posterior quantiles. Because $\\kappa$ acts only around $\\hat\\theta$, bias comparisons with the naive estimate reflect the posterior mean alone, whereas interval width and coverage reflect the posterior and $\\kappa$ jointly; we did not evaluate $\\kappa=1$.\n118:All baselines are computed by the evaluator from the same log. \\emph{Naive Wilson}: observed accuracy of $\\hat k$ with a 90\\% Wilson interval. \\emph{Bonferroni-Wilson}: the same estimate with the Wilson critical value taken at level $1-\\alpha/(2K)$. \\emph{Resampling}: 100 random half-splits; on each, the best configuration on one half is re-scored on the other, the mean score gap $g$ is rescaled to $\\hat p_{\\hat k}-g/\\sqrt2$, and a Wilson interval is placed around it.\n127:\\textbf{Cases and searchers.} Table~\\ref{tab:cases} lists the cases. \\emph{Exhaustive} search evaluates every configuration on every question and selects the highest total; \\emph{successive halving} works with a nominal budget of $N\\!\\cdot\\!K$ evaluations over $\\lceil\\log_2K\\rceil$ rounds, discarding the lower half of the survivors each round, so configurations eliminated early are observed on few questions and the log is sparse. Each case has $R=30$ replications ($R=10$ for Z1).\n264:\\caption{Width factor $\\kappa$ applied around the estimate in each evaluation case (computed from the reporter's own function). It is not used in Z1, where the all-zero rule applies.}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"50ad42a8-2b3f-48e6-bd4a-8d4c069e45a9","uuid":"26b74b74-d1fe-4692-930a-b375e331f011","timestamp":"2026-11-11T14:44:41.902Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"Supplementary Information validation failed: Supplementary Information must not import unchecked TeX files\n46:% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.combined_score]]\n98:% @evidence[[source_evidence/reporter_eb_irt/program.py]]\n147:% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.combined_score]]\n